X.append(P(f'Long-sequence models need memory that spans many timescales at low cost. We study the Horizon Transform (HT), a causal transform that represents a signal by the responses of a bank of damped complex oscillators whose horizons are spaced geometrically, so that a number of channels growing with the logarithm of the sequence length covers every timescale. HT is exactly invertible, has a bounded response, runs as an O(T) recurrence or an O(T log T) FFT convolution, and is related by exact identities to the sliding Fourier bin, the gammatone filter and diagonal state-space layers. An input-adaptive variant (HT-A) matches a selective state-space baseline on a recall task when its rotation is scaled by the adaptive step. In reduced-scale experiments on a CPU with synthetic data, the time-invariant HT is {np.median(e13["out"]["Self-attention"]["8192"]["t"])/np.median(e13["out"]["HT (FFT conv)"]["8192"]["t"]):.0f} times faster and {e13["out"]["Self-attention"]["8192"]["mem"]/e13["out"]["HT (FFT conv)"]["8192"]["mem"]:.0f} times smaller than one attention layer at 8,192 samples; the default bank attains the highest scale-free recall of nine banks (0.80 against 0.71 for the best state-space bank); placing channels on the signal band brings classification to within the noise of a Morlet bank (60.9% against 63.5%); and orthogonalizing the bank raises exact-recall capacity from 29 to 43 and scale-free recall from 0.80 to 0.88. We also report where the default bank is not competitive, in reconstruction against the STFT and wavelets and in exact recall, and we leave trained-model evaluation and the remaining open problems to future work.'))
X.append(P([('Keywords: ','b'),'causal transforms; multi-scale analysis; state-space models; long-sequence modeling; exact inversion; constant-Q analysis']))
# ============ 1 Introduction
X.append(H1a('1 Introduction'))
X.append(P(f'Many sequence tasks, from forecasting and audio to language, depend on information that arrives at very different timescales: a fraction of a second, a minute, a day. A model for them has to keep a summary of a long past at a cost that does not grow quadratically with the length of the sequence. Self-attention {c("attn")} gives every position direct access to every other, but its time grows with the square of the length, and exact attention needs careful engineering to avoid a quadratic memory footprint {c("flash")}. Recurrent networks keep a state of fixed size {c("lstm","gru")} but have difficulty learning dependencies across long spans {c("bengio")}. Structured state-space models (SSMs) {c("s4","s4d","s5","lru","mamba")} revived recurrence for long sequences through linear, diagonalizable dynamics whose parallel evaluation is a scan or a convolution {c("blelloch","martin")}, and selective variants let the input control what is remembered {c("mamba","ssd")}. Signal processing has a parallel tradition of causal banks of exponential filters: sliding Fourier analysis {c("jacobsen","swift")}, gammatone banks {c("slaney","gamma")} and Laguerre filters {c("laguerre")}.'))
X.append(P('This paper studies one simple member of this family, the Horizon Transform (HT). It can be pictured as a bank of tuning forks that ring and fade at different rates. Every channel keeps one complex number. At each new sample it multiplies the stored number by a factor slightly smaller than one in magnitude, which makes the channel forget, rotates it by a fixed angle, which gives the channel a preferred frequency, and adds the new sample. Channels differ only in their horizon h_k, which sets how fast they forget, and in their frequency ω_k. Horizons are spaced geometrically, 1, 2, 4, 8 and so on, so covering horizons from 1 to 4,096 takes 13 scales: the number of scales grows with the logarithm of the sequence length.'))
X.append(P('Three consequences follow from the recurrence. First, cost: an update takes a few arithmetic operations and one stored number per channel, and the same transform can be computed in parallel by a scan or by FFT convolution. Second, invertibility: each state equals the previous state times a known factor plus the new sample, so the sample can be recovered exactly from two consecutive states, and from noisy states by pooling channels. Third, differentiability: horizons, frequencies and gains are ordinary parameters that can be fixed by design or learned by gradient descent. An adaptive variant, HT-A, lets the current input set each channel’s forgetting rate and gain, so that a channel can hold a value across a long gap and release it when a relevant event arrives, which is the behavior selective state-space models exploit.'))
X.append(P(f'HT is a transform layer, not a complete model, and it is not new in all of its parts. Causal exponential kernels, multi-scale banks and diagonal complex recurrences all have precedent (Section 2), and a SWIFT bin {c("swift")} follows the same recurrence. The question we ask is what this particular combination buys. Our contributions are the following.'))
X.append(BL([('Definition. ','b'),'HT and HT-A with geometric horizons, unit-power normalization and, for the adaptive form, a step-scaled rotation (Section 3).']))
X.append(BL([('Inverse and properties. ','b'),'An exact inverse, least-squares and noise-weighted decoders for noisy coefficients, and a bounded response with a closed-form frame symbol, each checked numerically (Sections 3 and 6.1).']))
X.append(BL([('Exact relations. ','b'),'Identities with the sliding Fourier bin, the gammatone filter, constant-Q analysis and diagonal state-space layers (Section 4).']))
X.append(BL([('Protocol. ','b'),'A pre-specified protocol with baselines, metrics, three hypotheses and decision rules (Section 5).']))
X.append(BL([('Evidence. ','b'),'Reduced-scale results on cost, multi-timescale memory, spectral classification and adaptive recall (Section 6), and a repair for the weak exact recall of the default bank (Section 7).']))
X.append(BL([('Limits and future work. ','b'),'A stated account of where the default bank is not competitive, what was not tested, and the studies that would settle the open questions (Section 8).']))
X.append(P('Section 2 reviews related work, Section 3 defines HT, Section 4 relates it to existing transforms, Section 5 describes the protocol, Section 6 reports results, Section 7 reports repairs and design guidelines, Section 8 states limitations and future work, and Section 9 concludes.'))
# ============ 2 Related work
X.append(H1a('2 Related work'))
X.append(P([('Time–frequency analysis. ','b'),f'The short-time Fourier transform (STFT) {c("allen")} and Gabor expansions {c("gabor")} analyze a signal with one window at every frequency. Wavelets {c("daub","mallat","grossmann","torrence")} and the constant-Q transform {c("brown")} tie the window to the frequency. Frame theory {c("duffin","painless")} governs stable reconstruction from redundant coefficients, and matching pursuit {c("mpursuit")} selects atoms from a dictionary.']))
X.append(P([('Causal recursive banks. ','b'),f'The sliding DFT {c("jacobsen")} and the sliding windowed infinite Fourier transform (SWIFT) {c("swift")} update a Fourier bin recursively; the SWIFT bin uses a damped complex pole as HT does. Auditory models use gammatone filters {c("slaney")}, and the gamma and Laguerre models {c("gamma","laguerre")} use cascades of identical one-pole filters. Damped-sinusoid atoms with recursive filter banks were used for signal decomposition {c("goodwin")}.']))
X.append(P([('Recurrent memory. ','b'),f'Gated recurrent networks {c("lstm","gru")} address the difficulty of long dependencies {c("bengio")}. Reservoir computing {c("esn","lsm","jaeger02")} studies the memory of fixed random recurrences. Multi-timescale networks assign different update rates to units {c("clock","hier","tallec")}.']))
X.append(P([('State-space models. ','b'),f'HiPPO {c("hippo")} and Legendre Memory Units {c("lmu")} derive memory from polynomial projections. Structured state-space layers {c("lssl","s4","s5")}, their diagonal forms {c("dss","s4d","htyh")}, linear recurrent units {c("lru")}, an oscillatory state-space model {c("linoss")} and selective recurrences {c("mamba","ssd")} followed. Linear attention {c("linatt")}, RWKV {c("rwkv")}, retention {c("retnet")} and Hyena {c("hyena")} are related linear-time designs.']))
X.append(P([('Attention and convolution. ','b'),f'Attention {c("attn")} and its efficient variants {c("flash","long","big","reform","linf","perf")} trade accuracy or generality for cost. Dilated and global convolutions {c("dil","wavenet","tcn")} give long receptive fields. Learned front ends {c("davis","sinc","leaf")} replace fixed filterbanks. Long Range Arena {c("lra")} is the standard benchmark for long sequences, and simple baselines are strong in forecasting {c("zeng","informer","auto","hyndman")}.']))
X.append(P(f'A limited web search found no method published under the name Horizon Transform but did find the closely related constructions above, in particular SWIFT bins {c("swift")}, damped-sinusoid atoms {c("goodwin")} and the diagonal state-space layers {c("s4d")}. A systematic prior-art search has not been done.'))
