X.append(fig('fig_ch1_bank.png','Figure 1. The HT bank. The input feeds K channels, each a damped complex oscillator with its own horizon and frequency; the coefficients can be inverted exactly. In the adaptive variant HT-A the input also sets each channel’s step and gain (dashed).',5.5))
X.append(P('Figure 2 shows three channels applied to a synthetic signal of 1,024 samples that contains a slow oscillation with a period of 512 samples, a burst of an oscillation with a period of 16 samples between t = 300 and t = 420, three isolated spikes and noise. A channel with a short horizon (4 samples) reacts to each spike and returns to baseline within a few samples; a channel with horizon 32 and period 16 is nearly silent except during the burst, whose period matches its frequency, and it fades about one horizon after the burst ends; a channel with horizon 256 follows the slow oscillation and needs about one horizon to build up from its zero initial state. Rearranging the recurrence of the middle channel recovers the input at every sample with an error of about 2 × 10⁻¹⁵.'.replace('Figure 2',f'Figure {FN["example"]}')))
X.append(fig('fig_ch1_example.png','Figure 2. A synthetic input (top) and the magnitude of three HT channels: horizon 4 and period 4, horizon 32 and period 16, horizon 256 and period 512. An illustration of the mechanism, not a performance result.',5.6))
X.append(H2a('3.3 Exact inversion and noisy coefficients'))
