X.append(P('The theory of HT is complete for the time-invariant bank: the inverse is exact and the response is bounded. For HT-A, boundedness is established only under a sufficient condition (Eq. '+str(E['hta_b'])+'). Uniqueness of the HT-A inverse is open (no counterexample was found in 800 random trials), the frame bounds for time-varying coefficients are open, and so are an optimal bank under noise, the class of operators approximated by HT with a nonlinear readout (for which a related oscillatory model has a universality result '+c('linoss')+'), and generalization guarantees.'))
X.append(H2a('8.4 Future work'))
X.append(P('The studies are ordered in three stages with two parallel tracks. Stage 1 (feature banks) comprises the pilots reported in part above, and real, non-white inputs. Stage 2 (small trained models) comprises the initialization swap, in which the same layer is started from the HT, S4D and LRU initializations and compared on a small set of long-range tasks, a comparison of frequency placements, and the exact-recall hybrid. Stage 3 is the full protocol on Long Range Arena, forecasting benchmarks, audio and EEG, with trained baselines and at least ten seeds for the H1 comparisons. The two parallel tracks are theory (HT-A invertibility and frame bounds, optimal banks) and engineering (fused scan kernels and a GPU benchmark against FlashAttention). Each study has a decision rule and a pre-agreed response to a negative result; for example, if an LRU started from the HT initialization matches the HT layer, the gain is due to pole placement and the initialization becomes the contribution.'))
