# ============ 9 Conclusion
X.append(H1a('9 Conclusion'))
X.append(P('The Horizon Transform is a bank of damped complex oscillators with geometrically spaced horizons. It is exactly invertible, has a bounded response, and in its time-invariant forms is far cheaper than attention at long lengths, by a factor of 34 in time and 16 in memory at 8,192 samples in our CPU measurements. The default bank achieves the highest scale-free recall of nine banks, a band-matched placement brings spectral classification to the level of a Morlet bank, the adaptive variant matches a selective state-space baseline on a recall task once its rotation is scaled by the step, and orthogonalization raises the exact-recall capacity of the bank from 29 to 43 while keeping its multi-timescale advantage.'))
X.append(P('The evidence is reduced-scale and synthetic. The default bank does not match the STFT or wavelets in reconstruction at equal coefficient count, its exact recall is weak unless the bank is orthogonalized, and its lead in scale-free recall is modest. These limits, and the open questions on HT-A, are the subject of the studies outlined in Section 8.4. The most defensible contribution at present is the geometric placement of poles as an initialization, together with the repairs that make the bank usable, and the decisive next test is end-to-end training on long-range tasks with trained baselines.'))
